  "worked_solution": "For a plane lamina of mass $M$ and a line ${l}_{M}$ perpendicular to the lamina passing through its center of mass, the parallel axis theorem states that the moment of inertia of the lamina about a line $l$ parallel to ${l}_{M}$ is given by\n\n$$\n{I}_{l}={I}_{M}+{d}^{2}M,\n$$\n\nwhere $d$ is the distance between the lines, and ${I}_{M}$ and ${I}_{l}$ are the moments of inertia about the lines ${l}_{M}$ and $l,$ respectively.\n\nThe distance between the lines is $d=45\\,\\text{cm}=0.45\\,\\text{m}.$ Then, applying the parallel axis theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{l} & =1.75+(0.45)^{2}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =1.75+0.81 \\\\  & =2.56.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the moment of inertia is $2.56\\,kg\\cdot {m}^{2}.$",
